items/lem-characteristic-numbers-of-products-follow-the-whitney-sum-and-kunneth-formulas.md:82:3.1 The complex-manifold case. If $M$ and $N$ are complex manifolds, then $TM$ and $TN$ are complex vector bundles, and the complexification of an underlying real complex bundle is $V\oplus\overline V$: the map $v\otimes z\mapsto(zv,z\bar v)$ is complex linear, with inverse $(a,\bar b)\mapsto\frac{a+b}{2}\otimes1+\frac{a-b}{2i}\otimes i$, where $v\mapsto\bar v$ is the canonical antilinear copy. The formulas respect local frames. Thus $(TM_{\mathbb R})_{\mathbb C}\cong TM\oplus\overline{TM}$; by the conjugation formula of [F4], $c(\overline{TM})$ is obtained from $c(TM)$ by $c_i\mapsto(-1)^ic_i$, so the odd part of $c(TM\oplus\overline{TM})$ cancels in pairs and the correction of step 2.1 vanishes. Hence the integral class identity $p(T(M\times N))=p(TM)\times p(TN)$ holds for complex-manifold factors, and in particular for products of complex projective spaces. [F4, step 2.1]
items/thm-complex-algebraic-varieties-and-maps-admit-compatible-finite-stratifications.md:92:7.1 For the projective case factor $f$ as a closed algebraic embedding $i:X\hookrightarrow Y\times\mathbb P^N(\mathbb C)$ followed by the projection $\pi:Y\times\mathbb P^N(\mathbb C)\to Y$. Complex projective space is the continuous image of the unit sphere in $\mathbb C^{N+1}$ under $z\mapsto[z]$, hence compact by [F6]. A closed algebraic embedding is analytically closed, because in each chart its defining polynomial equations have continuous coefficients and their common zero set is closed. For a compact $C\subseteq Y$ the set $\pi^{-1}(C)=C\times\mathbb P^N(\mathbb C)$ is compact by [F6], and $f^{-1}(C)=i^{-1}(C\times\mathbb P^N(\mathbb C))$ is a closed subset of it, hence compact. Therefore $f$ is analytically proper, and step 6.1 applies; this is the analytic properness used by the consumers. [F6, step 6.1, algebra] ∎
