items/thm-schur-multiplier-of-an-abelian-group-is-its-exterior-square.md:51:2.1 The image of $[F,R]$ in $\bigwedge^2X$ is generated by $x\wedge r$ with $x\in X$ and $r$ in the image $K$ of $R$ in $X$. The alternating universal property identifies $(\bigwedge^2X)/(X\wedge K)$ with $\bigwedge^2(X/K)=\bigwedge^2A$. Hence $\Phi$ is injective as well as surjective. Since $[F,F]\le R$, [[thm-hopf-formula-for-the-schur-multiplier]] identifies $M(A)=(R\cap[F,F])/[F,R]=[F,F]/[F,R]$ under the stated hypotheses. Presentation maps preserve commutators and exterior products, and the canonical Hopf identification is natural, so the resulting isomorphism is natural in $A$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
items/lem-tensor-products-of-primitive-vectors.md:47:[F1] *Tensor coactions.* The representation/comodule dictionary applies to arbitrary vector spaces. For finite-dimensional pairs, [[lem-tensor-and-hom-representations-are-rational]] gives the tensor coaction explicitly. The same formula from the two finite coaction expansions on each elementary tensor is defined in arbitrary dimension by the tensor universal property; its counit and coassociativity identities follow from those of the two factors and multiplicativity of the Hopf-algebra counit and coproduct. This is verified directly in step 1.1. ([[def-rational-representation-and-comodule-of-an-affine-group-scheme]], [[thm-universal-property-of-module-tensor-products]])
items/post-fd-periodic-newtonian.md:24:  unforced periodic Leray–Hopf class. It asserts no empirical universality,
items/thm-logarithmic-complex-constructs-smooth-open-mixed-hodge-structures.md:70:[F4] Local analytic inputs ([[thm-dolbeault-lemma-polydisc]], [[thm-cohomological-kunneth-isomorphism-under-finite-free-hypotheses]], [[cor-homology-of-spheres]], [[cor-cohomology-over-a-field-is-dual-to-homology-over-that-field]], [[lem-locally-contractible-metric-spaces-have-singular-constant-sheaf-cohomology]]). On a polydisc $P\subseteq\mathbb C^m$, every $\bar\partial$-closed smooth $(p,q)$-form with $q\ge1$ is $\bar\partial$-exact on every smaller relatively compact polydisc. Holomorphic coefficients are continuous and separately holomorphic ([[prop-holomorphic-functions-are-continuous-and-separately-holomorphic]]), admit absolutely normally convergent power series on smaller polydiscs ([[thm-power-series-expansion-in-several-complex-variables]]), and these series can be differentiated termwise there ([[thm-power-series-define-holomorphic-functions-in-several-variables]]). For a product of circles the Kunneth theorem computes the cohomology as the exterior algebra on the degree-one generators, with each factor contributing one generator because the homology of the circle is $\mathbb Q$ in degrees $0$ and $1$; over the field $\mathbb Q$ the Kronecker evaluation identifies a cohomology group with the full dual of homology, so the classes are detected by their values on the torus cycles.
items/thm-logarithmic-complex-constructs-smooth-open-mixed-hodge-structures.md:94:3.1 (Every canonical truncation and the rational normalization.) Take the sheafification $\mathcal S_U^\bullet$ of smooth singular cochains on $U$. The flasque-resolution construction of [F6] applies to smooth simplices as well: barycentric subdivision and its prism operators preserve smoothness, local polydisc contractions are smooth, and the global small-simplex representative argument is unchanged. Thus $\mathcal S_U^\bullet$ resolves $\mathbb C_U$ and its terms are acyclic on every open subset. They are therefore $j_*$-acyclic, and $j_*\mathcal S_U^\bullet$ represents $Rj_*\mathbb C_U$. Integration of logarithmic forms on smooth simplices in $U$, with the cochain map and normalization of [F2], gives the chain map $L\to j_*\mathcal S_U^\bullet$. On the polydiscs of step 2.2 the target stalk cohomology is the cohomology of $(\Delta^*)^s\times\Delta^{d-s}$, the exterior algebra on meridians by [F4]. Integration gives $\int_{\gamma_i}e_j=2\pi i\delta_{ij}$, so the comparison is an isomorphism on every cohomology sheaf. Let $\tau_{\le a}$ denote the canonical truncation, with degree $a$ equal to the kernel of the next differential. Since every degree-$k$ logarithmic form has at most $k$ poles, the identity gives a genuine chain inclusion $\tau_{\le a}L\to W_aL$. By step 2.2 it induces an isomorphism in degrees at most $a$ and both sides have no cohomology above $a$, hence is a quasi-isomorphism for every $a$. These inclusions commute with the maps as $a$ increases. We therefore have the filtered comparison zigzag $(Rj_*\mathbb Q_U\otimes\mathbb C,\tau)\to(Rj_*\mathbb C_U,\tau)\leftarrow(L,\tau)\to(L,W)$, all arrows graded quasi-isomorphisms; exact scalar extension and the local finite torus cohomology establish the first arrow. On each graded sheaf the rational meridian wedge corresponds to $(2\pi i)^{-a}e_I$, which pairs by $1$ with the degree-$a$ meridian torus, not a degree-$2a$ class. Ordered residues thus identify $R^aj_*\mathbb Q_U$ with $i_{a*}\mathbb Q_{D^{[a]}}$ and place its comparison lattice in $(2\pi i)^{-a}\mathbb Q$, exactly the rational Tate normalization $(-a)$. [F2, F4, F6, step 2.1, step 2.2, construct, algebra]
items/thm-logarithmic-complex-constructs-smooth-open-mixed-hodge-structures.md:96:4.1 (Actual filtered global-section models and a single comparison.) The sheaf comparison in step 3.1 is a zigzag, so it must not be treated as a nonexistent direct sheaf map in the opposite direction. Resolve the complexes and their filtrations by the exact Godement construction: $C^0(A)(V)=\prod_{x\in V}A_x$, with the natural embedding and successive cokernels. Stalks and products are exact for vector spaces under AC. Moreover $C^0(A)=\prod_{x\in X}i_{x*}A_x$ is an injective sheaf of field-vector spaces: each $A_x$ is injective over its field by extension of linear maps along a chosen complement, the exact stalk functor is left adjoint to $i_{x*}$, and a product of injectives is injective because the component extension maps can be chosen simultaneously under AC. The successive cokernel construction therefore gives an actual injective resolution. Exactness of $C^0$ and the short exact augmentation sequences give exactness of each successive cokernel functor by the snake lemma, so the resulting functorial resolution is exact on filtration levels, their finite intersections and both associated gradings; its global-section total computes derived global sections. Apply it to $L$ with $W,F$ and to the other weight-filtered models in the zigzag. Replace the rational and comparison models by $\tau_{\le d}$ first; their cohomology sheaves above $d$ vanish by the local torus calculation, so this is a quasi-isomorphism and leaves a finite weight range $0\le a\le d$. After resolution and global sections the complex-coefficient arrows in the zigzag are maps of weight-filtered vector-space complexes with graded quasi-isomorphisms. For the rational scalar-extension arrow, the global graded comparison is the isomorphism $H^{r-a}(D^{[a]};\mathbb Q)\otimes\mathbb C\to H^{r-a}(D^{[a]};\mathbb C)$ from [F3], with the normalization of step 3.1; this proves it is a graded quasi-isomorphism without interchanging tensor product with an infinite product of stalks. Such a map admits a weight-filtered homotopy inverse: its cone has acyclic weight-graded complexes; a cycle in $W_a$ has a leading graded preimage, and subtracting its differential lowers the cycle to $W_{a-1}$, so finite induction supplies a preimage in $W_a$. Thus the cone is acyclic with a $W$-strict differential. Choose filtration-compatible complements of its cycles and right inverses of its differential; the resulting degree-minus-one homotopy contracts the cone and preserves $W$, and the cone homotopy equations give a $W$-filtered homotopy inverse of the original map. AC permits these linear complements. Inverting the leftward comparison in step 3.1 and composing the remaining arrows gives an actual $W$-filtered chain map $K_{\mathbb Q}\otimes\mathbb C\to K_{\mathbb C}$, with $K_{\mathbb C}=\Gamma(X,C^\bullet(L))$ carrying the original $W,F$. Its graded maps are quasi-isomorphisms. Different inverse choices are filtered-homotopic, so their induced comparison and image filtrations on cohomology agree. These are bounded-below degreewise-finite filtered models of the type required in [F5]. [F6, A1, step 3.1, construct, algebra]
items/lem-whitney-sum-coalgebra-is-well-defined-on-stable-thom-cohomology.md:61:1.1 Embed the first ambient coordinate space in the odd coordinates and the second in the even coordinates. Their orthogonal sum defines $\mu_{p,q}:BO(p)\times_k BO(q)\to BO(p+q)$, with the fiber isometry $(v,w)\mapsto J_{\rm odd}v+J_{\rm even}w$. On every pair of finite Grassmannian stages these are continuous frame and bundle maps. Every compact Hausdorff test into the product projects into finite base subcomplexes, hence finite Grassmannian stages; the compactly generated map-out test proves continuity globally. The fiber isometry and the radial product-disk homeomorphism from the prespectrum construction give a continuous based map $T_p\wedge T_q\to T_{p+q}$. No additional product-base paracompactness or CW-type assertion is needed. To apply the Whitney and external Thom-class formulas within their stated scope, first restrict to finite Grassmannian stages, whose product is a finite compact Hausdorff CW complex by the characteristic-product-disk attachment test. There both formulas apply to the actual bundles with their canonical mod-two orientations. These finite-stage identities determine the global cohomology identity: every singular cycle in the base product, or in the relative product used for the Thom smash, projects into finite stage subcomplexes by compact support. The natural field evaluation isomorphism (also for relative free singular chains, by cohomological UCT over the field) detects equality on these cycles. Kification leaves the compact singular simplices and their homotopies unchanged. Thus the finite-stage normalized Thom and Whitney identities hold globally and the computation is independent of the classifying choices. At finite ranks, all mod-two cohomology groups in a fixed degree are finite-dimensional: the Thom isomorphism identifies them with homogeneous pieces of F₂[w₁,…,w_p], and the Schubert CW model has finitely many cells in each degree. The Thom CW structure therefore has finite-dimensional cellular chains in each degree, so its mod-two homology is finite-dimensional and free in each degree. The passage from relative product to smash also has an explicit good-pair check. On each positive-rank Thom space let $\lambda=1-\|v\|$ off the basepoint and $\lambda(*)=0$, a continuous quotient function; at rank zero $T_0=S^0$ use values zero and one. Choose a continuous cutoff $c:[0,1]\to[0,1]$ equal to one on $[0,1/4]$ and zero on $[1/2,1]$. In the product, the open neighborhood $\delta=\min(\lambda(x),\lambda(y))<1/4$ of the closed wedge retracts onto the wedge by the homotopy that replaces each deficiency $\lambda_j$ by $\lambda_j-t\delta c(\lambda_j)$, retaining its base and fiber direction. This increases the fiber norm without exceeding one, collapses a coordinate of minimum deficiency at time one, and fixes the wedge since there $\delta=0$. At a zero vector the cutoff is zero, so the formula is continuous there; the disk/sphere quotient and compact tests establish continuity everywhere, including the collapsed basepoint. The rank-zero case is fixed throughout. The good-pair quotient theorem and field UCT therefore identify relative product cohomology with reduced smash cohomology; kification leaves the singular complexes unchanged. Now the relative field Künneth theorem for the CW pairs (T_p,*) and (T_q,*) gives H̃^{p+q+d}(T_p∧T_q;F₂) ≅ ⊕_{a+b=d} H̃^{p+a}(T_p;F₂)⊗H̃^{q+b}(T_q;F₂). Only finitely many summands occur, since M has no negative degrees. [given, F1, F2, F3]
items/lem-whitney-sum-coalgebra-is-well-defined-on-stable-thom-cohomology.md:63:2.1 w_k U ↦ Σ_{i+j=k}(w_iU)⊗(w_jU), U ↦ U⊗U. Indeed, the base class pulls back by w_k(E⊕F)=Σw_i(E)w_j(F), and the Thom class pulls back to the external product of the two normalized Thom classes. This formula is independent of the chosen classifying maps. In the following square, μ_{p,q} also denotes the induced based Thom multiplication T_p∧T_q→T_{p+q}; its cohomology pullback is the rankwise map just computed. Compatibility with rank transitions is the cohomological commutativity of this square for stabilization of the first factor: H̃^{p+q+1+d}(T_{p+q+1}) --μ_{p+1,q}^*--> H̃^{p+q+1+d}(T_{p+1}∧T_q) | ρ_{p+q}(d) | ρ_p(a)⊗id v v H̃^{p+q+d}(T_{p+q}) --μ_{p,q}^*----> H̃^{p+q+d}(T_p∧T_q), where on a Künneth summand a+b=d, the right vertical map applies ρ_p(a) to the first factor and the identity to the second. On bundles, the two composites classify respectively (ε¹⊕γ_p)⊕γ_q and ε¹⊕(γ_p⊕γ_q); associativity and the coordinate permutation of the ordered direct sum give the bundle isomorphism over fixed-coordinate stabilization. It preserves mod-two Thom normalization, and the Whitney formula leaves each w_i unchanged under adjoining the trivial line (with indices above a finite rank truncated to zero). Naturality and homotopy uniqueness of the classifying maps therefore make the square commute. The same argument stabilizes the second factor. Consequently the rankwise pullbacks define a map Δ_M:M^d→⊕_{a+b=d}M^a⊗M^b. [step 1.1, F1, F2]
