items/thm-quiver-quotient-stack-borel-moore-operation-package.md:63:4.1 This also proves that these constructions do not depend on cover or presentation. Any two finite choices are compared by their product; on that product the maps are ordinary unit/counit traces and positive complex smooth orientations, and the comparisons are natural in these maps. For a fixed finite collection of maps or equations, choose all frame gaps greater than the finitely many bounded-Hom comparison bounds of their inputs, outputs, units, counits and orientation maps. All objects descend from bounded constructible coefficients on the fixed finite presentation varieties. Faithfulness then reflects the finite-stage equality. One does not choose one N for every degree. For a pasted pair of Cartesian squares, use the same target frame cover, add the product of the finitely many source frame spaces, and pull back the proper arrows rather than adding independent frames to them. This realizes all corners of the pasted diagram as ordinary Cartesian squares simultaneously. The ordinary six-functor theorem says their exchange maps paste: expanding them as adjunction mates reduces the equation to the composition of units/counits and the triangle identities. The product-resolution comparisons preserve those units and counits by their common principal-bundle identity. Therefore (4) respects Cartesian pasting, composition of proper maps, composition of block-smooth maps, identities and external products. For external products use product frame covers; orientations concatenate in the displayed order and their real degrees are even. No unproved stack formalism, transversality, or finite-dimensional cohomology of an infinite classifying space is used. [F2, F3, F4, step 3.1]
items/lem-hopf-ideal-kernels-and-quotients.md:63:1.2 (Part (b).) Let $\mathfrak a\subseteq A$ be a Hopf ideal and let $q\colon A\to A/\mathfrak a$ be the quotient map. Since $\Delta(\mathfrak a)\subseteq\ker(A\otimes_kA\to A/\mathfrak a\otimes_kA/\mathfrak a)$, there are unique $k$-algebra homomorphisms $\bar\Delta\colon A/\mathfrak a\to A/\mathfrak a\otimes_kA/\mathfrak a$, $\bar\varepsilon\colon A/\mathfrak a\to k$ and $\bar S\colon A/\mathfrak a\to A/\mathfrak a$ with $\bar\Delta q=(q\otimes q)\Delta$, $\bar\varepsilon q=\varepsilon$ and $\bar Sq=qS$, by the universal property of the quotient ring [F2] and because $q\otimes q$, $\varepsilon$ and $qS$ kill $\mathfrak a$. The three Hopf identities for $(\bar\Delta,\bar\varepsilon,\bar S)$ hold because they hold for $(\Delta,\varepsilon,S)$ and $q,q\otimes q,q\otimes q\otimes q$ are surjective; this makes $A/\mathfrak a$ a Hopf algebra with $q$ a morphism, and any Hopf structure with that property must satisfy the three displayed identities, so it is unique. If $g\colon A\to C$ is a Hopf morphism with $\mathfrak a\subseteq\ker g$, then $g$ induces $\bar g\colon A/\mathfrak a\to C$ with $\bar gq=g$ by [F2]; since $q$ is surjective and $g$ preserves $\Delta,\varepsilon,S$, so does $\bar g$, and $\bar g$ is the unique such map. [F1, F2]
items/thm-quiver-flag-relative-position-strata-give-a-faithful-geometric-pbw-basis.md:90:1.1 For $a:L_\eta\to L_\nu[n]$ and $f:L_\nu\to\omega_E^G[m]$, set $a\cdot f=f[n]\circ a$. Associativity of composition makes $\bigoplus_\nu\operatorname{Ext}_G(L_\nu,\omega_E^G)$ a left module for the opposite-composition product. Adjunction and dualizing transitivity [F2] give $\operatorname{Ext}_G^n(L_\nu,\omega_E^G)=H_{d_\nu-n}^{\mathrm{BM},G}(X_\nu;k)=H_G^{d_\nu+n}(X_\nu;k)$, giving the stated shift; the diagonal and Chern cap classes act by identity and cup product. Fiber scaling contracts the vector bundle $X_\nu$ equivariantly onto $F_\nu=G/B_\nu$. Here $B_\nu$ is the product of triangular stabilizers, and $B_\nu/T$ is its unitriangular group, homeomorphic to a complex affine space through its matrix entries. The Borel maps $EG/T\to EG/B_\nu$ have that contractible fiber, so pullback identifies $H_G(F_\nu;k)$ with $H_T(\mathrm{pt};k)$. This comparison can be made on the finite resolutions [F10], whose fiber cohomology is $k$ in degree zero: the pullback is an isomorphism by the stalkwise fiber computation and [F2]. For $T=(\mathbb C^*)^m$, the rank-one resolutions in [F10] have bases $(\mathbb{CP}^{N-1})^m$. Repeated [F5] and [F6] give the basis of products of tautological powers with each exponent less than $N$; all degrees stabilize as $N$ increases [F7, F10]. Thus the stable ring is $k[x_1,\ldots,x_m]$, with the flag quotient lines corresponding to the individual torus characters. This proves the polynomial model without asserting a polynomial extension of $H_G(\mathrm{pt};k)$. [F1, F2, F3, F5, F6, F7, F10, given]
items/thm-finite-thom-detector-is-a-mod-two-cohomology-isomorphism-below-2r.md:62:4.1 H̃^k(P_r;F₂) ≅ ⊕_{b∈B(r), d_b≤e} A^{e−d_b}. Here finite-product Künneth applies: each factor has finite-dimensional F₂ homology in every degree by the finite-type lemma, hence finite-free homology over F₂; the polynomial presentation of the Eilenberg–Mac Lane cohomology gives the stated cohomology basis. The classifying-map evaluation identity gives $f_r^*(a(\iota_{r+d_b}))=a(m_{r,b})$. [step 3.1, F2, F3]
items/cex-changing-a-framing-can-change-the-pontryagin-thom-class.md:71:2.1 The unit circle agrees with the quotient-circle model of the supplied universal cover by [[thm-real-line-mod-integers-is-homeomorphic-to-the-unit-circle]]. For $j\ge2$, $S^j$ is path-connected, locally path-connected (small chart balls suffice), and simply connected. The subgroup criterion [[thm-covering-space-lifting-criterion]] therefore lifts each based map $S^j\to S^1$ to $\mathbb R$. Linear contraction of the based lift gives $\pi_j(S^1)=0$. The long exact sequence of the Hopf bundle consequently makes $h_*:\pi_3(S^3)\to\pi_3(S^2)$ an isomorphism, since the adjacent groups $\pi_3(S^1)$ and $\pi_2(S^1)$ vanish. Degree identifies $\pi_3(S^3)$ with $\mathbb Z$ and its identity with $1$, so $[h]=h_*[\mathrm{id}]$ generates $\pi_3(S^2)$ and is nonzero. [step 1.1, algebra]
