items/thm-the-formal-quantum-serre-half-embeds-in-the-quantum-shuffle-algebra-and-is-degreewise-free.md:133:3.1 Put $J=\ker p$ and $\mathfrak j=J\cap\mathfrak b^+$. As the kernel of a Hopf map, $J$ is a two-sided ideal and has zero counit. For $x\in J$, $(p\otimes p)\Delta(x)=0$; right exactness of tensor products and surjectivity of $p$ give $\ker(p\otimes p)=J\otimes U\mathfrak b^++U\mathfrak b^+\otimes J$, so $J$ is a coideal. Under the stated AC hypothesis, apply [F4] to obtain $J=U\mathfrak b^+\mathfrak j=\mathfrak j U\mathfrak b^+$ with $\mathfrak j$ a Lie ideal. The universal property [F15] identifies the quotient $U\mathfrak b^+/J$ with $U\mathfrak a$, where $\mathfrak a=\mathfrak b^+/\mathfrak j$, and $p$ with the quotient map. AC is used here only through [F4]; all other choices below are finite-dimensional or explicitly specified. [F4, F9, F15, F16, step 2.1, algebra]
