## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** AC, $r\geq0$, the fixed product torus presentation, the smooth manifold $M$, and its smooth left $T$-action.

[A1] Assume AC. It supplies the classifying-space coefficient calculation and implies the countable-choice hypothesis in the imported fundamental-field definition. [[def-axiom-of-choice]] [[def-countable-choice]]

[F1] For the selected product Milnor model, $H^*(BT;\mathbb R)\cong\operatorname{Sym}_{\mathbb R}(\mathfrak t^*)$ with the stated degree-two normalization. [[lem-torus-classifying-space-cohomology]]

[F2] A smooth left action is a jointly smooth map satisfying the left action law, so each $a_t$ is a diffeomorphism. [[def-smooth-left-action-of-a-lie-group]]

[F3] The fundamental field is $X_\xi(p)=\left.\frac{d}{ds}\right|_0\exp_T(-s\xi)\cdot p$; its flow is $p\mapsto\exp_T(-s\xi)\cdot p$. [[def-fundamental-vector-field-of-a-left-action]]

[F4] A smooth differential $k$-form is a smooth section of $\bigwedge^kT^*M$; the exterior derivative maps $\Omega^k(M)$ to $\Omega^{k+1}(M)$. [[def-smooth-differential-k-form]] [[def-exterior-derivative-by-the-invariant-vector-field-formula]]

[F5] Singular cohomology with real coefficients is the cocycle quotient of the real singular cochain complex and is a graded ring under cup product. [[def-singular-cohomology-with-coefficients]]

[F6] The exterior derivative satisfies $d^2=0$. [[thm-the-exterior-derivative-squares-to-zero]]

[F7] The interior product is pointwise insertion of the vector field into the first slot of an alternating form; hence two insertions anticommute. [[def-interior-product-of-a-form-by-a-vector-field]]

[F8] The Lie derivative of a form is the derivative of pullback along the vector field's flow. [[def-lie-derivative-of-a-differential-form]]

[F9] For every smooth vector field $X$ and form $\alpha$, $\mathcal L_X\alpha=d(\iota_X\alpha)+\iota_X(d\alpha)$. [[thm-cartans-magic-formula]]

## Proof

1.1 Since each polynomial generator has degree two and $d$ raises form degree by one, both summands in $d_T$ raise total degree by one. Under a change of basis of $\mathfrak t$, the polynomial basis and vector-field basis transform contragrediently, so $\sum_i u_i\otimes X_{e_i}$ and therefore $d_T$ are unchanged. [given, F3, F4, algebra]

1.2 The exterior derivative commutes with pullback. For $t\in T$, the left action law and commutativity give $a_t\circ a_{\exp(-s e_i)}=a_{\exp(-s e_i)}\circ a_t$; differentiating at $s=0$ shows $(a_t)_*X_{e_i}=X_{e_i}$. Thus contraction by each $X_{e_i}$ preserves invariant forms, while $u_i$ is fixed by the coadjoint action. Both terms of $d_T$ preserve the diagonal $T$-fixed subspace, so $d_T:C_T(M)	o C_T(M)$ is well-defined. [F2, F3, step 1.1]

1.3 On polynomial-valued forms, expand the square as
$$d_T^2=d^2-\sum_i u_i(d\iota_{X_{e_i}}+\iota_{X_{e_i}}d)+\sum_{i,j}u_iu_j\iota_{X_{e_i}}\iota_{X_{e_j}}.$$
The first term is zero by [F6]. The last sum is zero because $u_i u_j=u_j u_i$ while insertions into alternating forms anticommute by [F7]. The middle sum is $-\sum_i u_i\mathcal L_{X_{e_i}}$ by [F9]. Hence $d_T^2\alpha=-\sum_i u_i\mathcal L_{X_{e_i}}\alpha$ for every $\alpha\in C_T(M)$. [F6, F7, F9, step 1.1, algebra]

2.1 For an invariant form $\alpha$, every pullback $(a_{\exp(-s e_i)})^*\alpha$ equals $\alpha$. By [F3] this one-parameter family is the flow of $X_{e_i}$, so differentiating at $s=0$ and using [F8] gives $\mathcal L_{X_{e_i}}\alpha=0$ for each $i$. Substitution in step 1.3 proves $d_T^2\alpha=0$ on $C_T(M)$. [F3, F8, step 1.3]

3.1 If $r=0$, the sum in $d_T$ is empty and $d_T=d$, whose square is zero by [F6]. If the action is trivial, every $X_{e_i}=0$ and again $d_T=d$ on invariant forms. If $M=\varnothing$, both the form complex and the Borel construction are empty/zero, so no exception to well-definedness occurs. No compactness of $M$, symplectic form, or Hamiltonian hypothesis was used. [F2, F3, F6, step 2.1] ∎

