+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** AC, $r\geq0$, the fixed product torus presentation, the smooth manifold $M$, and its smooth left $T$-action.
+
+[A1] Assume AC. It supplies the classifying-space coefficient calculation and implies the countable-choice hypothesis in the imported fundamental-field definition. [[def-axiom-of-choice]] [[def-countable-choice]]
+
+[F1] For the selected product Milnor model, $H^*(BT;\mathbb R)\cong\operatorname{Sym}_{\mathbb R}(\mathfrak t^*)$ with the stated degree-two normalization. [[lem-torus-classifying-space-cohomology]]
+
+[F2] A smooth left action is a jointly smooth map satisfying the left action law, so each $a_t$ is a diffeomorphism. [[def-smooth-left-action-of-a-lie-group]]
+
+[F3] The fundamental field is $X_\xi(p)=\left.\frac{d}{ds}\right|_0\exp_T(-s\xi)\cdot p$; its flow is $p\mapsto\exp_T(-s\xi)\cdot p$. [[def-fundamental-vector-field-of-a-left-action]]
+
+[F4] A smooth differential $k$-form is a smooth section of $\bigwedge^kT^*M$; the exterior derivative maps $\Omega^k(M)$ to $\Omega^{k+1}(M)$. [[def-smooth-differential-k-form]] [[def-exterior-derivative-by-the-invariant-vector-field-formula]]
+
+[F5] Singular cohomology with real coefficients is the cocycle quotient of the real singular cochain complex and is a graded ring under cup product. [[def-singular-cohomology-with-coefficients]]
+
+[F6] The exterior derivative satisfies $d^2=0$. [[thm-the-exterior-derivative-squares-to-zero]]
+
+[F7] The interior product is pointwise insertion of the vector field into the first slot of an alternating form; hence two insertions anticommute. [[def-interior-product-of-a-form-by-a-vector-field]]
+
+[F8] The Lie derivative of a form is the derivative of pullback along the vector field's flow. [[def-lie-derivative-of-a-differential-form]]
+
+[F9] The exterior derivative is a degree-one graded derivation of the algebra of forms. [[thm-the-exterior-derivative-is-a-graded-derivation]]
+
+[F10] Contraction by a vector field is a degree-minus-one graded antiderivation. [[prop-interior-product-is-a-graded-antiderivation]]
+
+[F11] For every smooth map $F$, $d(F^*\omega)=F^*(d\omega)$. [[thm-the-exterior-derivative-commutes-with-pullback]]
+
+[F12] For every smooth vector field $X$ and form $\alpha$, $\mathcal L_X\alpha=d(\iota_X\alpha)+\iota_X(d\alpha)$. [[thm-cartans-magic-formula]]
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 Since each polynomial generator has degree two, $d$ raises form degree by one, and contraction lowers form degree by one, both summands in $d_T$ raise total degree by one. By [F9] and [F10], each summand is a degree-one derivation because each $u_i$ has even degree. Under a change of basis of $\mathfrak t$, the polynomial basis and vector-field basis transform contragrediently, so $\sum_i u_i\otimes X_{e_i}$ and therefore $d_T$ are unchanged. [given, F4, F5, F7, F8, F9, F10, algebra]
+
+1.2 Pullback naturality [F11] shows that $d$ preserves the $T$-fixed forms. For $t\in T$, the left action law and commutativity give $a_t\circ a_{\exp(-s e_i)}=a_{\exp(-s e_i)}\circ a_t$; differentiating at $s=0$ shows $(a_t)_*X_{e_i}=X_{e_i}$. By the pointwise definition [F8], contraction by each $X_{e_i}$ preserves invariant forms, while $u_i$ is fixed by the coadjoint action. Both terms therefore preserve the diagonal $T$-fixed subspace, so $d_T:C_T(M)\to C_T(M)$ is well-defined. [F2, F3, F8, F11, step 1.1]
+
+1.3 On polynomial-valued forms, expand the square as
+$$d_T^2=d^2-\sum_i u_i(d\iota_{X_{e_i}}+\iota_{X_{e_i}}d)+\sum_{i,j}u_iu_j\iota_{X_{e_i}}\iota_{X_{e_j}}.$$
+The first term is zero by [F6]. The last sum is zero because $u_i u_j=u_j u_i$ while insertions into alternating forms anticommute by [F8]. The middle sum is $-\sum_i u_i\mathcal L_{X_{e_i}}$ by [F12]. Hence $d_T^2\alpha=-\sum_i u_i\mathcal L_{X_{e_i}}\alpha$ for every $\alpha\in C_T(M)$. [F6, F8, F12, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+2.1 Put $\phi_s=a_{\exp(-s e_i)}$. The one-parameter subgroup law gives $\phi_{s+h}=\phi_s\circ\phi_h$, and differentiating at $h=0$ gives $\frac{d}{ds}\phi_s(p)=(d\phi_s)_pX_{e_i}(p)=X_{e_i}(\phi_s(p))$ by step 1.2. Thus $\phi_s$ is the flow of $X_{e_i}$. For an invariant form $\alpha$, $\phi_s^*\alpha=\alpha$ for every $s$; differentiating at zero and using [F8] gives $\mathcal L_{X_{e_i}}\alpha=0$. Substitution in step 1.3 proves $d_T^2\alpha=0$ on $C_T(M)$. [F3, F8, step 1.2, step 1.3]
+
+3.1 If $r=0$, the sum in $d_T$ is empty and $d_T=d$, whose square is zero by [F6]. If the action is trivial, every $X_{e_i}=0$ and again $d_T=d$ on invariant forms. If $M=\varnothing$, both the form complex and the Borel construction are empty/zero, so no exception to well-definedness occurs. No compactness of $M$, symplectic form, or Hamiltonian hypothesis was used. [F2, F3, F6, step 2.1] ∎
