Assume the full Axiom of Choice. For $r\geq0$, let
$T=(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z)^r$ with the quotient topology and coordinatewise
addition. On each circle factor, for every open interval $I\subset\mathbb R$
of length less than $1$, use the chart
$q(I)\to I$ given by the unique lift through the quotient map
$q:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R/\mathbb Z$; give $T$ the product smooth structure.
Write $\mathfrak t=T_0T$. For $1\leq i\leq r$, let $e_i$ be the tangent vector
at $0$ to the one-parameter subgroup
$s\mapsto(0,\ldots,[s],\ldots,0)$ in the $i$th coordinate. These vectors form
the period-one basis of $\mathfrak t$, and $e_i^*$ denotes its dual basis.
The standard Lie exponential is normalized by
$\exp_T(s e_i)=(0,\ldots,[s],\ldots,0)$ for every $s\in\mathbb R$.
For $r=0$, use the one-point group and the empty basis. This convention makes
$T$ a compact connected abelian Lie group, hence a torus in the sense of the
standard compact-Lie-group definition; its identity inclusion is a cofibration,
items/ex-qftx-equivariant-cohomology-of-a-free-circle-action.md:82:[F7] The period-one tangent generator exponentiates as $s\mapsto[s]$. [[def-standard-product-torus-as-a-compact-lie-group]]
items/ex-qftx-equivariant-cohomology-of-a-free-circle-action.md:90:2.1 Integer translations preserve $dx$, so $-dx$ descends through the standard quotient charts to a global smooth one-form $\eta$ on the circle. The circle translations act transitively, so an invariant smooth function is constant. Every smooth one-form is $f(x)dx$ for a smooth periodic function $f$; invariance under all translations forces $f$ constant. Since higher forms vanish on a one-dimensional manifold, the invariant form algebra is spanned by $1$ and $\eta=-dx$. By [F7], the negative-exponential flow is $[x]\mapsto[x-s]$, hence $X_e=-\partial_x$ and $\iota_{X_e}\eta=1$; also $d\eta=0$. Substitution in [F1] gives $d_TP(u)=0$ and $d_T(P(u)\eta)=-uP(u)$. [F1, F5, F6, F7, given]
items/def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model.md:172:[F7] Singular cohomology with this cup product is a unital graded ring. [[prop-cup-product-is-natural-unital-and-associative]]
items/def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model.md:176:[F9] The interior product is pointwise insertion of the vector field into the first slot of an alternating form; hence two insertions anticommute. [[def-interior-product-of-a-form-by-a-vector-field]]
items/def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model.md:194:[F18] The fixed product torus is a compact connected abelian Lie group, and each period-one basis vector exponentiates to its coordinate circle subgroup as specified in the local torus definition. [[def-standard-product-torus-as-a-compact-lie-group]]
items/def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model.md:200:1.1 The right diagonal action is a group action by homeomorphisms, so its orbit quotient $ET\times_T M$ is a topological space. By [F5]–[F7] its real singular cohomology has the stated cup-product ring structure; at $M=\{\ast\}$ the quotient is $BT$, whose coefficient ring is identified by [F1]. [A1, F1, F5, F6, F7, given]
items/def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model.md:202:1.2 Pullback by each $a_{t^{-1}}$ preserves wedge products: evaluating on tangent vectors shows that pulling back the pointwise alternating product gives the product of the pullbacks. Thus the diagonal $T$-action is by algebra automorphisms and its fixed subspace is closed under the multiplication defined by the algebraic tensor-product rule and [F15]–[F16]. Since each polynomial generator has degree two, $d$ raises form degree by one, and contraction lowers form degree by one, both summands in $d_T$ raise total degree by one. By [F11] and [F12], each summand is a degree-one derivation because each $u_i$ has even degree. Under a change of basis of $\mathfrak t$, the polynomial basis and vector-field basis transform contragrediently; linearity in [F3] then makes $\sum_i u_i\otimes X_{e_i}$ basis-independent, so $d_T$ is unchanged. [F2, F3, F4, F9, F11, F12, F15, F16, algebra]
items/def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model.md:206:1.4 Pullback naturality [F13] shows that $d$ preserves the $T$-fixed forms. For $t\in T$, the left action law and commutativity give $a_t\circ a_{\exp(-s e_i)}=a_{\exp(-s e_i)}\circ a_t$; differentiating at $s=0$ shows $(a_t)_*X_{e_i}=X_{e_i}$. By the pointwise definition [F9], contraction by each $X_{e_i}$ preserves invariant forms, while $u_i$ is fixed by the stipulated trivial action on the polynomial factor. Both terms therefore preserve the diagonal $T$-fixed subspace, so $d_T:C_T(M)\to C_T(M)$ is well-defined. [F2, F3, F9, F13, F18, step 1.2, step 1.3]
items/def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model.md:210:The first term is zero by [F8]. The last sum is zero because $u_i u_j=u_j u_i$ while insertions into alternating forms anticommute by [F9]. The middle sum is $-\sum_i u_i\mathcal L_{X_{e_i}}$ by [F14]. Hence $d_T^2\alpha=-\sum_i u_i\mathcal L_{X_{e_i}}\alpha$ for every $\alpha\in C_T(M)$. [F8, F9, F14, step 1.2, algebra]
items/def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model.md:212:2.1 Put $\phi_s=a_{\exp(-s e_i)}$. The local torus definition [F18] identifies $\exp(-s e_i)$ with the coordinate one-parameter subgroup, so the group law gives $\phi_{s+h}=\phi_s\circ\phi_h$. Differentiating at $h=0$ gives $\frac{d}{ds}\phi_s(p)=(d\phi_s)_pX_{e_i}(p)=X_{e_i}(\phi_s(p))$ by step 1.4. Thus $\phi_s$ is the flow of $X_{e_i}$. For an invariant form $\alpha$, $\phi_s^*\alpha=\alpha$ for every $s$; differentiating at zero and using [F10] gives $\mathcal L_{X_{e_i}}\alpha=0$. Substitution in step 1.5 proves $d_T^2\alpha=0$ on $C_T(M)$. [F3, F10, F18, step 1.4, step 1.5]
items/def-standard-product-torus-as-a-compact-lie-group.md:119:[F7] An inclusion of a relative CW subcomplex has the homotopy extension property and is a cofibration. [[prop-relative-cw-inclusions-are-cofibrations]]
